          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life and Education</strong><br>\nJoan Miró was born in 1893 in Barcelona, Spain. He initially pursued a career in business before enrolling at the art academy at La Llotja in his hometown. After a nervous breakdown, he abandoned business and devoted himself entirely to art, later moving to Paris to join the emerging modern art scene.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Surrealist Association</strong><br>\nAlthough Miró never formally joined the Surrealists, his work was heavily influenced by the movement. His dreamlike paintings, characterized by vivid colors and fantastical imagery, aligned with Surrealism’s goal to express the subconscious mind.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Development of a Unique Style</strong><br>\nMiró’s work evolved from the detailed, realistic depictions of Catalan landscapes to surreal, abstracted forms. By the 1930s, his style had become more symbolic and poetic, often depicting a mature combination of abstract elements with suggestive, organic forms.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
