          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Late Career and Public Works</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Miró continued to explore large-scale and public works. His murals, tapestries, and sculptures are prominent in various public spaces, reflecting his desire to make art accessible to a broader audience.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Beliefs and Artistic Vision</strong><br>\nMiró maintained a strong belief in his Catalan identity and the universal language of art. He often spoke of his work as anti-painting, challenging traditional painting techniques and the commercialization of art.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nJoan Miró passed away in 1983, leaving behind a vast legacy that influenced generations of artists. His imaginative approach to form and color, and his commitment to individual expression, remain influential in contemporary art.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
